2026-09-23T12:08:50.4380608Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4380691Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4380771Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4380853Z     Features
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4380942Z     --------
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4381133Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4381626Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4381708Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4381806Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382108Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382211Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382296Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382574Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382745Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4382831Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383097Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383201Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383277Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383378Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383460Z     
2026-09-23T12:08:50.4383932Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
